#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""lagoon — a small floating mermaid portal.

commands:
  serve          run the daemon (unix socket + http on 127.0.0.1)
  panel [names]  open panel window(s); each given name pins that chart, none = live stream
  push [name]    push mermaid from stdin to the portal
  show <name>    bring a named chart back to the portal
  list           list charts (* marks current)
  compare <a> <b> [...]  open a split compare of charts on the portal (persists as a tab)
  unmerge <a+b>  remove a persisted merged view
  offsets <name> print a chart's stored drag nudges
  history [name] print recent pushes (all charts, or one)
  open           open the portal in the default browser

panel: one tab per chart name (click or keys 1-8, 9 = last, • = changed while away);
       drag pans, pinch / ctrl+wheel or + - zooms, 0 or double-click fits,
       arrows walk the current view's history, Escape jumps to latest, s saves a PNG
       to ~/Downloads, c copies the mermaid source, and the bar offers copy mmd /
       copy png / save png / open file, x resets manual layout. dragging a node (or a
       subgraph's empty area) nudges it; edges and arrowheads follow, and nudges
       persist per chart; double-click an edge's text label to edit it (enter
       saves, clear-all + enter removes). shift+drag on empty space
       rubber-band-picks several cells to drag as one group; Esc or a plain
       click clears the pick. ctrl+tab / ctrl+shift+tab cycle the tab strip
       (wrapping, merged tabs included); arrows walk the current view's history.
       lagoon panel a b (or the merge button: tabs become selectors, then
       "merge (N)" / cancel) opens a split compare — nodes only in a pane
       tint that pane's color; "end merge" or Escape returns. Merge supports
       2+ charts.

agents / cli can drive it directly, no lagoon binary needed:
  unix socket   ~/.local/state/lagoon/lagoon.sock — one request per connection:
                 first line: "push <name>" | "show <name>" | "list" |
                 "compare <a> <b> [...]" | "offsets <name>" | "history [name]",
                 then mermaid text for push, then half-close:
                 printf 'graph TD\nA-->B\n' | nc -U ~/.local/state/lagoon/lagoon.sock
  filesystem    ~/.local/state/lagoon/current.mmd — write mermaid here; polled and ingested

env: LAGOON_DIR (default ~/.local/state/lagoon), LAGOON_PORT (default 52466)
"""
